#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d53ec9d-95fb-5513-858f-df463994d752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381437} {:answer/id #uuid "9691b759-2ef0-5670-95c6-cd9a1faf1987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381438} {:answer/id #uuid "6a207ff4-9d19-529c-be44-9f7988292c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381439} {:answer/id #uuid "31ec6334-543d-51b6-bde3-c3312180d507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381440} {:answer/id #uuid "19df01e9-bc45-554b-973a-a8ede24bfcf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31ec6334-543d-51b6-bde3-c3312180d507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381440} :answer-field/id #uuid "4414f573-e5d7-5cc6-a225-160f076aea94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdf49813-6e1d-544d-9586-e910e685c090" :question/math-academy-id "q-127142" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=7\\,i+2\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $6.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =7, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(7)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2)\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }42\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(42\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0-12)-(0-12) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdd15b4-4647-5846-b84f-4454255a45c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381444} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecdb90f-aa0c-5e89-b583-08d4f0f26aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381445} {:answer/id #uuid "fea33c99-91e5-54cd-bdf4-6f8d017f2c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381446} {:answer/id #uuid "c6985e92-1c06-53a2-8cd0-e93c1b5e02a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381447} {:answer/id #uuid "dca1e8ca-1421-52dd-9f11-40b9deff6c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6985e92-1c06-53a2-8cd0-e93c1b5e02a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381447} :answer-field/id #uuid "8625364a-fb2f-5290-a22d-9ebb59bb878e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2901633-3db4-52cc-b894-08113fb1d31b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127148" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=8\\,i-(x+3)\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $2.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =8, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+3=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for sine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e22f257-68d1-5db0-9b76-b9b083579afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381451} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6541eb-515a-5427-b591-6a85f75c00dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381452} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9886ea-d577-5f47-958f-2ba6b985c0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f96ca02-2b4b-5da3-b224-9ebb462fbdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381454} {:answer/id #uuid "815c012c-d1fc-5eff-a635-9cd24f7fa5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e22f257-68d1-5db0-9b76-b9b083579afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381451} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c488a8-7d7f-56c8-bc4d-15f98dc65fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18503f26-a22e-52cf-b6b1-0a0a3543137f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127151" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074644/assets/0ea0204d3e1f230efcde4b82da36b1986af732cdf2a01ad868aa0f6bf7414326.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4y\\,i+(2x-1)\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074644/assets/59e503e0c4d950f030891bbaeb7ed890426ef470a1e1dae89d27eabb8dd591a6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4y=4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x-1=2{t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2}-1)\\cdot (2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t-4{t}^{3}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t-4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-{t}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-1)-(0-0){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275757e7-70d4-5803-8fd6-e451a7155453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381458} {:answer/id #uuid "5277d7f2-62e8-5721-80d2-e5597127afba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381459} {:answer/id #uuid "c013ab39-434e-5d8a-a72f-f6a845549b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381460} {:answer/id #uuid "73db3b9e-6e3e-56c3-adf2-217c52e3c8f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381461} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8c9cc0-a657-5b5d-b3a8-a90b4240d832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73db3b9e-6e3e-56c3-adf2-217c52e3c8f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381461} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d8fd5f-8b44-5b2a-b356-45230c2f676e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16f99b81-68b5-508d-b9ab-28041a83e857" :question/math-academy-id "q-127244" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074644/assets/6043a1f88474e2c282938d3b3f0193002b6e11b5fb6d16292fea43d63292d1b0.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y={x}^{3}$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-3{x}^{2}\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074644/assets/1d54fe3c07f04a7d05567942e3eea6efaf1cb516070a5d7cc31ef726c0ddb423.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-3{x}^{2}=-3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3{t}^{2})\\cdot (3{t}^{2})-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{t}^{4}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{5}-1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c745374-c649-54c8-8155-75e672ffbe12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381465} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2f03a2-c36a-5ed1-9826-16061edeec48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381466} {:answer/id #uuid "823a8421-d3ef-585d-acc0-74170b3b5bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381467} {:answer/id #uuid "eda34f3b-cfd4-508d-9bc9-e2a6260e3ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381468} {:answer/id #uuid "d1737850-2f3f-541f-9806-16f2c2764994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c745374-c649-54c8-8155-75e672ffbe12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381465} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bafbe7f-e2e9-56f1-bc0d-3407e9208b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3cdfb68c-2147-539b-92b7-7e15857ce98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127254" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074644/assets/67dca8f0202d0c5d1041935a6aee7c4ebd0e3c6c449f2ee1a5ff24c1f381410c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{e},1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074644/assets/08fc4561a322b690da7bd28a9e69a91c687256e8a902ec7690dda5e6a1c689a6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =0, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2{y}^{2}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=-\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(0)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}){|}_{1/e}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
